                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "TL;DR: Firewatch is a beautiful, short narrative game with a believable central relationship and a mystery that becomes genuinely gripping for a while. The buildup worked better than the payoff for me, but it’s an easy recommendation if you enjoy walking simulators and story-focused games. 7/10.\r\n\r\nThe first thing that stands out is how beautiful it is. The forests, mountains, sunsets and colors give the whole game a distinct identity. Just walking around is pleasant.\r\n\r\nAnd walking is most of what you do. Firewatch is very much a walking simulator: explore the park, climb or rappel when needed, inspect things, and talk to Delilah over the radio. Navigation is probably the closest thing it has to a gameplay system, where you use a map and compass to work out where you are instead of simply following a marker. It’s simple, but it works.\r\n\r\nThe story starts surprisingly heavy. That opening hits hard, although the game never reaches that emotional weight again. Henry’s relationship with Delilah works much better throughout. After months of talking over the radio, their connection feels believable, and by the end I did care about them.\r\n\r\nThe central mystery was the strongest part for me. Around the middle of the game I wanted answers immediately. Unfortunately, the explanation didn’t fully live up to that buildup. The conclusion itself wasn’t necessarily bad, but parts of the whole situation felt a little too hard to believe.\r\n\r\nThe dialogue choices also seem to just deciding how Henry responds than actually changing anything meaningful. And, because the game only lasts around four hours, its lack of actual gameplay never became boring or repetitive. It tells its story, does what it wants to do, and gets out.",
